From bef7327677f350e1f33ca76430a0cfdb538da586 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Brian Bockelman <bbockelman@morgridge.org>
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 19:36:53 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 9/9] [#66] [XrdPfc] Service open requests on a timer during
 the initial scan

While the ResourceMonitor's initial namespace scan runs, every File::Open
queues itself and blocks until the scan thread has cross-checked the
file's directory. The scan thread only drained that queue once every 100
directories traversed. On a large cache under heavy load, 100 directories
(or a single huge one) can take minutes to stat, so every new open waits
for minutes and the cache stops serving. A production cache was observed
with ~2850 threads parked in CrossCheckIfScanIsInProgress across three
stack captures while the scanner sat in one directory read.

Service the queue on a time basis (at most every 100ms) instead, from
two places on the scan thread: between directories, and every 1024
entries while reading a single directory. To make the mid-directory
checkpoint safe, the out-of-band directory scan now uses a private
FsTraversal rather than the main traversal's buffers. Each checkpoint
services only the requests present when it starts, so a steady stream
of opens can no longer starve the scan (and keep the gate closed).

The accounting guarantee is unchanged: a waiter is released only after
its directory's baseline usage has been recorded, and all DirState tree
access stays on the scan thread.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
